{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for a sign upon your hand.</b> Our Sages, may their memory be blessed, said (Menachot 37a) 'the weaker hand' — to teach that the laying of tefillin is on the left hand. And the reason for this is that the intellect and the matter are adversaries to one another: when this one rises, that one falls. And as they afflicted the matter, so the intellectual power increased. And the reason for the affliction of Egypt was in order to bring the matter of Israel into the iron crucible, and through this the intellect would be established and gain mastery. And elders together with youths will testify to this, for while a person is still a youth and uncultured and the power of his matter is at its strength, then the power of his intellect is weak; and when he advances in years and the power of his matter steadily diminishes, his intellect gains mastery. Therefore one who says that the former days were better than these (Ecclesiastes 7:10) — you did not ask this on account of wisdom, for after all, ״בִּישִׁישִׁים חָכְמָה וְאֹרֶךְ יָמִים תְּבוּנָה״ (Job 12:12)." "And so it is with a person's two hands: for the left hand is the weaker one, and it is feeble (כֵּהָה) opposite the heart, in which the dwelling-place of wisdom resides — for the intellectual locus in the heart causes his hand to be feeble, because the hand is preoccupied with the implements of matter. But the right hand is in the vicinity of the liver, for there is the dwelling-place of the desiring faculty, and it does not oppose the hand, for there is the main source of its strength. But in the place of the dwelling of the intellect, there the hand is feeble. And when he says here ״וְהָיָה לְאוֹת עַל יָדְכָה״ (Exodus 13:16), it is as if he said that it shall be for a sign upon your heart, for the heart is the cause of the feebleness of the hand. For the reason for tefillin is to be for a person a reminder in the place of the dwelling of the intellect — in the brain and in the heart. And regarding the head-tefillin he said that they shall be ״בֵּין עֵינֶיךָ״ (Exodus 13:16), for ״עֵינָא וְלִבָּא תְּרֵי סַרְסוּרֵי דְּחִטְאָה נִינְהוּ״ — 'the eye and the heart are two agents of sin' (Yerushalmi Berakhot 1:5). Therefore these two places require something that reminds them of the existence of HaShem, blessed be He, and His power, and through this they will turn back from transgression." "Another matter: therefore he mentioned 'the weaker hand' to teach that not by his own strength does a man prevail, for ״לַה׳ הַמִּלְחָמָה״ — 'the battle is HaShem's.' For a person's hand is feeble and weak to do anything great or small, were it not that the hand of HaShem empowers the one who holds onto it. As it is written (Psalms 16:8) ״שִׁוִּיתִי ה׳ לְנֶגְדִּי תָמִיד״ — 'I have set HaShem before me always,' etc. For at first David was distressed about the placement of the heart on the left and the liver on the right, as though it were the will of El that the desiring faculty be primary and the intellect in the heart secondary, just as the right hand is primary and the left secondary. But afterward he said: ״שִׁוִּיתִי ה׳ לְנֶגְדִּי תָמִיד״ — and then my left hand is opposite the right of HaShem, and in this I trust: ״כִּי מִימִינִי בַּל אֶמּוֹט״ — 'at my right hand, I shall not be shaken,' that there shall be no power in the desire that resides at the right to shake me from the path of the upright. For ״יְּמִין ה׳ רוֹמֵמָה״ — 'the right hand of HaShem is exalted' — to grant might and strength to the intellectual heart that stands opposite it, when my face is turned toward HaShem. Therefore ״שָׂמַח לִבִּי״ — 'my heart rejoices' — when I see that HaShem supports my hand. And this is what he concludes by saying: ״נְעִימוֹת בִּימִינְךָ נֶצַח״ — 'in Your right hand are pleasures everlasting' — meaning in Your right hand and not in mine, for everything depends on the right hand of HaShem, for it is opposite my feeble hand. And therefore HaShem commanded to lay the tefillin, in which the Name of HaShem is engraved, upon the feeble hand, to teach that the strong right hand of HaShem is what gives strength to this feeble hand. This is what it says: ״כִּי בְּחֹזֶק יָד הוֹצִיאָנוּ ה׳ מִמִּצְרָיִם״ — 'for by strength of hand HaShem brought us out of Egypt' — that is, the strong right hand of HaShem, which is opposite a person's left hand, and with it HaShem brought us out. And when my face is toward HaShem, then His right is opposite my feeble hand. And to teach this, He implanted in their hearts that no deed depends on a person's own action, but only on the help of HaShem, blessed be He." "And as for what is said ״וְהָיָה לְךָ לְאוֹת עַל יָדְךָ״ — 'and it shall be for a sign upon your hand' — but regarding the head-tefillin he did not mention 'for you,' because they are a sign also to others, as it is said (Deuteronomy 28:10) ״וְרָאוּ כָּל עַמֵּי הָאָרֶץ כִּי שֵׁם ה׳ נִקְרָא עָלֶיךָ וְיָרְאוּ מִמֶּךָּ״, 'and all the peoples of the earth shall see that the Name of HaShem is called upon you, and they shall fear you.' And our Master said: these are the tefillin of the head (Berakhot 6a). And the reason that one lays the hand-tefillin first and afterward the head-tefillin, and when removing them one removes the head-tefillin first, is because there are three agents of transgression: the eye sees, the heart desires, and the instruments of action complete (Rashi on Numbers 15:39). If so, the hand-tefillin deal with the majority, for there are both the heart and the hand; and therefore that place requires guarding from sin more than the head-tefillin do. Therefore one puts it on first to rectify that place, and likewise during removal they remain on the hand continually."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {2 "The closing phrase ״כִּי בְּחֹזֶק יָד הוֹצִיאָנוּ ה׳ מִמִּצְרָיִם״ does not appear verbatim at Exodus 13:16; the Kli Yakar is quoting or paraphrasing from elsewhere (cf. Exodus 13:3, 14:13, and similar locutions)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:06.060031194Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}